# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dotfiles are ignored by default, and allowed back in one by one.
#
# The default-deny is deliberate: a stray `.env`, `.idea/`, `.vscode/`, editor
# swap file or tool cache is easy to commit by accident and occasionally
# embarrassing. Opting each one in means nothing hidden reaches a commit without
# somebody deciding it should.
#
# Adding a dotfile that belongs in the repo? Add a `!` line for it here, or git
# will silently ignore it. `git check-ignore -v <path>` says which rule applies.
#
# Note the trailing slash on directories: git does not descend into an ignored
# directory, so `!.github/` has to un-ignore the directory itself before
# anything inside it can be considered.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.*
!.gitignore
!.github/
!.php-cs-fixer.dist.php
!.golangci.yml
!.goreleaser.yml

# Dependencies and lock files. The packages are libraries, so their locks are
# not committed; each consumer resolves its own.
/vendor/
/packages/*/vendor/
composer.lock
packages/*/composer.lock
node_modules/

# Generated artefacts: the spec and the documentation pages are built, never
# committed. CI publishes them.
packages/*/build/

# Local docs previews. These point at the docs router's proxy rather than the
# real API, so they only mean anything behind `composer docs:preview` and must
# never be shared as if they were the real pages.
packages/*/build/*-preview.html

# Go / Terraform provider
packages/terraform-provider-domeneshop/terraform-provider-domeneshop
packages/terraform-provider-domeneshop/dist/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*

TODO.md

# Key material. The dotfile default-deny above does not catch these — a GPG
# export or a GitHub App private key lands as `private.asc` or `app.pem`, with
# no leading dot. Generate them outside the working tree if you can; this is the
# backstop for when you don't.
*.asc
*.gpg
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
*.key
!**/testdata/**/*.key
